1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,310
Now we turn to partnership with school.
Keep that home observation in

2
00:00:08,310 --> 00:00:08,840
mind.

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,480
Your young person, your family and the
school may each hold different

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:13,780
parts of the picture.

5
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:18,670
Bringing those together can help shape
useful support, with your young

6
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,760
person as a partner in the conversation.

7
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:24,660
People arrive with very different school
experiences.

8
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:27,500
Some relationships feel helpful, and
others have been difficult.

9
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:31,220
You can take part without sharing a
personal account.

10
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:35,550
Please keep staff, schools and young
people unidentifiable in group

11
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:36,140
discussion.

12
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:41,670
Look at the overlapping partnerships:
family, school staff and support

13
00:00:41,670 --> 00:00:42,520
staff, with your young

14
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:44,520
person at the centre.

15
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:47,760
Each person brings different knowledge and
a different relationship.

16
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:49,920
Who else belongs in that picture for your
family:

17
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:53,680
perhaps a grandparent, therapist, tutor or
employer?

18
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,640
What is the shared purpose of these
partnerships?

19
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:00,600
How can your young person's own
communication help shape it?

20
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:04,160
Take a moment to reflect or discuss, and
pause if you'd like more time.

21
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,160
Keep your young person at the centre as
you consider the answer.

22
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:19,160
No single person holds the whole picture.

23
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:22,630
A useful partnership helps people
understand each other's knowledge and

24
00:01:22,630 --> 00:01:23,200
agree on support

25
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,880
that makes a difference to the young
person.

26
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:30,400
The Family-School Partnerships Framework
recognises parents and

27
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,960
families as young people's first

28
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,520
and continuing educators.

29
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:38,470
Learning happens across settings, and a
family's diversity and strengths

30
00:01:38,470 --> 00:01:40,280
matter to that learning.

31
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:43,960
The other principles include community
involvement, mutual trust and

32
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:45,680
shared responsibility, supported

33
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:48,280
by committed and collaborative leadership.

34
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:52,220
In an everyday conversation, look for
respectful listening, clear

35
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,200
information, your young person's

36
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:58,520
views and agreed next steps.

37
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:01,600
Family knowledge and school knowledge are
both needed from the

38
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:02,400
beginning.

39
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,890
Respectful listening is possible even when
people see a situation

40
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:05,440
differently.

41
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:10,000
What makes collaboration work?

42
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,040
Think of a situation where people have
worked well together,

43
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:16,040
inside or outside school.

44
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:19,240
What helped them listen and make a
decision?

45
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:22,520
Share a brief idea or reflect privately.

46
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,440
Pause here if you would like time.

47
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:36,730
The slide brings together shared goals and
values, mutual trust,

48
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:38,440
consultation, curiosity

49
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:40,640
and attention to different perspectives.

50
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,560
Your young person's voice belongs
throughout.

51
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:47,210
The phrase assume good intent may feel
difficult if trust has been

52
00:02:47,210 --> 00:02:47,960
damaged.

53
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:51,840
Trust needs action from both sides, and it
can take time to rebuild.

54
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:55,590
A possible opening is: how could we work
together so school goes well

55
00:02:55,590 --> 00:02:56,240
for my young

56
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:57,240
person?

57
00:02:57,240 --> 00:03:01,480
That gives the conversation a purpose
while leaving room to discuss

58
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:02,840
what needs to change.

59
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:06,280
With several teachers involved, it can
help to be clear about who will

60
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:07,120
carry the agreed

61
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:08,600
information between people.

62
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:13,760
There are also responsibilities that sit
behind these conversations.

63
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:18,540
The Disability Discrimination Act protects
people from discrimination

64
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:21,560
because of disability, including in
education.

65
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:25,930
The Disability Standards for Education
explain obligations to support

66
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:29,220
access and participation on the same basis
as students without

67
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:30,180
disability.

68
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:34,840
Schools must consult the student, or an
associate such as a parent or

69
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:38,640
carer, when considering reasonable
adjustments.

70
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:42,440
Useful questions are: how will we be
consulted?

71
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:44,760
What adjustments are in place?

72
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:46,840
How and when will they be reviewed?

73
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:51,370
This is general information, rather than
advice about an individual

74
00:03:51,370 --> 00:03:52,760
legal situation.

75
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:56,360
The Department of Education's
plain-language resources explain these

76
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:58,780
rights and responsibilities for families.

77
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:02,640
They are a useful place to return to after
the workshop.

78
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:07,810
In secondary school, ask early about
adjustments for assessments and

79
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:08,460
exams,

80
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:11,240
especially as the senior years approach.

81
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:17,480
Power can sit in the way a meeting is
arranged, the language used and

82
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:18,440
who can make decisions.

83
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:23,730
Familiarity with school systems, cultural
and historical experiences of

84
00:04:23,730 --> 00:04:24,360
education,

85
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:29,700
language and financial circumstances can
also shape how a conversation

86
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:30,200
feels.

87
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:36,790
Who sets the agenda? Whose knowledge gets
heard? Is your young person

88
00:04:36,790 --> 00:04:38,120
included in a way that works

89
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:44,130
for them? What might need to change about
the process? Naming these

90
00:04:44,130 --> 00:04:45,480
features can help us plan

91
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:49,220
a more balanced conversation. We can
examine the meeting itself without

92
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:50,760
turning the discussion

93
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:55,240
into a judgement about an identifiable
person.

94
00:04:56,920 --> 00:05:01,960
Use the Power dynamics activity on page
fifteen, or three sticky notes.

95
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:05,440
First, describe an imbalance from a home
perspective.

96
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:08,360
On the second, consider one from a school
perspective.

97
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:12,760
On the third, suggest how the imbalance
could be addressed.

98
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:16,000
Thinking about another perspective doesn't
mean excusing poor practice.

99
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,960
It helps us identify what could change in
a conversation.

100
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:24,280
You can share at your table, add anonymous
notes under Home, School

101
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:27,560
and Solutions, or keep your notes private.

102
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:28,840
Pause now for the activity.

103
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:40,200
Look again at the solutions. Could the
agenda be shared earlier?

104
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:42,040
Could language be clearer,

105
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:44,160
a support person be included,

106
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:45,720
or another way of contributing

107
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:47,240
be available?

108
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:50,920
Choose one idea that would make
participation more workable.

109
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:53,160
Keep it for the conversation plan later in
this session.

110
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,080
We're going to hear from Adon.

111
00:05:56,080 --> 00:06:00,660
As you watch, pay attention to what
decisions were made and who made

112
00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:01,000
them.

113
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,060
Notice the effect of being included, or
not included, in decisions about

114
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:07,440
your own experience.

115
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:04,840
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon?

116
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:09,160
Make a note on page fifteen, or discuss
the question briefly.

117
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:14,500
Then consider how your young person's
views are asked for and heard at

118
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:14,920
school.

119
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:17,620
Pause here if you'd like more time.

120
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:30,040
Your young person's views can be spoken,
written, typed, drawn or

121
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:32,160
shared through a trusted person.

122
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:33,920
Ask what helps,

123
00:08:33,920 --> 00:08:37,520
what is hard and what they want teachers
to understand.

124
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:41,040
Taking their views seriously supports
self-advocacy,

125
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:44,480
including when their answer is different
from what adults expected.

126
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:50,760
Consultation is more useful when there is
a clear shared purpose, open

127
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:51,720
questions and more

128
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:54,360
than one way to contribute.

129
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:58,800
Your knowledge and your young person's
knowledge deserve attention.

130
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:02,830
What was heard needs to be recorded
accurately, followed by an agreed

131
00:09:02,830 --> 00:09:04,320
action, responsibility

132
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:06,560
and follow-up.

133
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:10,480
The checklist on page sixteen can help
before and after a meeting.

134
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:13,800
Ask, listen and follow up.

135
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:17,620
Before leaving, check who will do what and
when you will hear how it is

136
00:09:17,620 --> 00:09:18,240
going.

137
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:21,380
A conversation becomes more useful when
everyone understands the next

138
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:21,680
step.

139
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:26,620
Secondary schools have more people and
systems to navigate.

140
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:32,780
A home-room teacher, pastoral care teacher
or year coordinator may help

141
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:34,100
with everyday matters.

142
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:38,840
Learning support or an inclusion team may
coordinate adjustments.

143
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:43,060
Wellbeing staff or a school counsellor may
support wellbeing.

144
00:09:43,060 --> 00:09:46,160
Names and responsibilities differ between
schools.

145
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:49,560
On page sixteen, note who you would
contact and how.

146
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:52,580
If you're unsure, mark the question to ask
school.

147
00:09:53,180 --> 00:09:57,480
It can help to agree one main contact, and
learn the portal or app

148
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:58,560
alongside your young person.

149
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:01,480
Pause while you make a start.

150
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:12,660
You don't have to complete every name now.

151
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:15,260
Start with the person who knows your young
person well

152
00:10:15,260 --> 00:10:18,260
and ask how the school organises the rest.

153
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:21,100
A short written follow-up after a
conversation

154
00:10:21,100 --> 00:10:24,340
can help everyone check what was agreed
and who will act.

155
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:29,880
Taking part in a meeting can happen in
different ways. Your young person

156
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:31,840
might attend, write something beforehand,

157
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:36,920
use their communication tools or choose a
trusted adult to share their

158
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:37,840
views.

159
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:41,110
Preparation matters. Ask what they want
said, what they'd like to say

160
00:10:41,110 --> 00:10:44,840
themselves and how they can signal that
they need a break.

161
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:49,220
Participation can grow over time,
including choosing their own agenda

162
00:10:49,220 --> 00:10:49,840
items.

163
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:53,630
It should be supported and chosen. If they
don't want to attend, their

164
00:10:53,630 --> 00:10:55,840
perspective can still be included.

165
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:59,840
If they say no to a support, that view
belongs in the discussion too.

166
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:03,840
This is practice in having a say about
their learning and their life.

167
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:10,040
The Working Together Communication Tool,
on the left, helps you agree how

168
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:10,880
home and school

169
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:12,680
will communicate.

170
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:17,430
You can choose a method, such as email,
phone or a communication book,

171
00:11:17,430 --> 00:11:18,840
then record how often,

172
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:22,760
what you'll communicate about, and the
next dates or steps.

173
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:26,920
The Partnership Planning Template, on the
right, asks what has worked,

174
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:28,280
what has been challenging

175
00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:30,400
and what could help.

176
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:34,360
You can use those prompts before a meeting
or complete them together.

177
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:37,360
A tool gives the conversation a place to
start.

178
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:40,840
Keep listening and your young person's own
communication alongside it.

179
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:48,920
The N C C D Meetings Tool Kit is a free
resource for families and schools.

180
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:52,400
It can help clarify the purpose of a
meeting about adjustments,

181
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:56,080
what information to bring, how
consultation will happen

182
00:11:56,080 --> 00:11:59,040
and what needs to happen afterwards.

183
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,600
The link is at the back of your workbook.

184
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:03,880
Schools follow their own system's
processes,

185
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:08,480
so ask who is responsible for each step
and how follow-up will happen.

186
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:11,480
The tool kit gives you a way to prepare
questions and understand what to

187
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:12,520
expect before,

188
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:13,960
during and after the meeting.

189
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:17,760
There are supports beyond this workshop.

190
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:21,440
Autism Connect provides autism information
and support.

191
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:25,620
Disability Gateway helps people find
disability information and

192
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:26,220
services.

193
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:28,720
Carer Gateway supports carers.

194
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:31,960
The contact details are at the back of
your workbook.

195
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:36,980
For young people, Kids Helpline offers
counselling for ages five to twenty-five
on

196
00:12:36,980 --> 00:12:40,760
eighteen hundred, fifty-five, eighteen
hundred.

197
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:46,090
Its website also has parent information
and directs adult parents to

198
00:12:46,090 --> 00:12:48,140
Parentline for parenting counselling.

199
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:53,450
Headspace offers support for ages twelve
to twenty-five, with information for
family

200
00:12:53,450 --> 00:12:54,360
and friends too.

201
00:12:54,360 --> 00:12:58,820
Lifeline's crisis support number is
thirteen, eleven, fourteen.

202
00:12:58,820 --> 00:13:01,900
Take a moment to note the services
relevant to you.

203
00:13:01,900 --> 00:13:05,900
If you need support, reaching out is a
practical next step.

204
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:15,280
You don't need to know every answer about
life after school.

205
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:19,600
Starting conversations in the middle years
allows more time to explore

206
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:20,560
interests,

207
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:23,680
subject choices and possible pathways.

208
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:26,320
Include your young person at every step.

209
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:29,920
Options can include further study,
training, work,

210
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:33,280
supported employment and community
participation.

211
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:37,040
Different support needs deserve equally
thoughtful planning.

212
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:40,960
Careers staff, learning support and
relevant disability or employment

213
00:13:40,960 --> 00:13:41,520
services

214
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:44,400
can help clarify what is available.

215
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:46,000
Keep questions open:

216
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
what matters to your young person,

217
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,800
what would they like to explore, and what
support would make that

218
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:52,560
possible?

219
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:56,470
An interest can offer a starting point
without becoming a decision made

220
00:13:56,470 --> 00:13:57,600
for them.

221
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:03,760
Turn to page seventeen and choose one
purposeful conversation with school.

222
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:05,640
What needs to be understood?

223
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:08,040
What question could open the conversation?

224
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:12,280
How will your young person take part, or
have their views included?

225
00:14:12,280 --> 00:14:17,340
What could be decided together, and how
will you follow up?

226
00:14:17,340 --> 00:14:18,840
Use short notes.

227
00:14:18,840 --> 00:14:22,880
Bring in one idea from the power activity
that could make the

228
00:14:22,880 --> 00:14:24,260
conversation more balanced.

229
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:27,220
There is no need to share a sensitive
situation.

230
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:30,220
Pause here and give yourself time to
prepare.

231
00:14:30,220 --> 00:14:33,790
Plan to talk with your young person about
the conversation before it

232
00:14:33,790 --> 00:14:34,480
happens.

233
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:46,880
Circle the question you want to open with.

234
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:49,840
Keep these notes for Session Six.

235
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:52,600
A small, specific next step is enough:

236
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:54,760
one thing that needs to be understood,

237
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:57,240
and a way to hear from your young person
as part of it.
